      <!-- 1. GREAT FIRE OF LONDON (Merged by Wynn & Yitao)          -->
      <c level="item" id="se-1666-fire">
        <did>
          <unittitle>Report from the London Gazette on the Great Fire of London</unittitle>
          <unitid>se-1666-fire</unitid>
          <unitid localtype="tna-reference">ZJ 1/1</unitid>
          <!-- RESEARCH & MERGE RATIONALE: 
               Wynn originally dated this to the specific day of the report (1666-09-02). 
               However, Yitao researched that newspaper editions from this era were often 
               compiled over a month. We decided to adopt Yitao's broader month-level date 
               (1666-09) to be more historically accurate to the publication cycle. -->
          <unitdatestructured unitdatetype="inclusive">
            <datesingle standarddate="1666-09">September 1666</datesingle>
          </unitdatestructured>
          <origination>
            <corpname><part>The London Gazette</part></corpname>
          </origination>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Wynn provided a very clear visual description of the digitised object. 
               We kept this because it helps users understand they are looking at a 
               photograph of a 350-year-old physical newspaper, not a modern reprint. -->
          <physdescstructured physdescstructuredtype="materialtype" coverage="whole">
            <quantity>1</quantity>
            <unittype>report</unittype>
            <physfacet>A photo taken from a newspaper aged more than 3 centuries ago</physfacet>
          </physdescstructured>
          <repository>
            <corpname><part>The National Archives</part></corpname>
          </repository>
          <langmaterial>
            <language langcode="eng">English</language>
          </langmaterial>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               We chose Yitao's abstract because it perfectly summarizes the *content* 
               (the first hours of the fire and the King's response) without getting 
               bogged down in 17th-century spelling. -->
          <abstract>A newspaper report on the first hours of the Great Fire of London and the response ordered by the king.</abstract>
          <dao daotype="derived" href="https://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ukgwa/+/https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/significant-events/great-fire-of-london-1666/" actuate="onrequest" show="new"/>
        </did>
        <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
             We kept Wynn's full transcript. In archival description, transcribing the 
             original text (including the original spelling like "brake out") is a core 
             accessibility requirement for users who cannot read the scanned image. -->
        <scopecontent>
          <head>Transcript</head>
          <p>London, Sept. 2</p>
          <p>About two a clock this morning a sudden and lamentable fire brake out in this City, beginning not far from Thames Street, near London Bridge; which continues still with great violence, and hath already burnt down to the ground many houses thereabouts; Which said accident affected His Majesty with that tenderness and compassion that He was pleased to Himself in Person with His Royal Highness to give order that all possible means should be used for quenching the fire, or stopping its further spreading. In which care, the Right Honourable the Earl of Craven was sent by His Majesty, to be more particularly assisting to the Lord Major and Magistrates; and several companies of His Guards sent into the City, to be helpful what ways they could in so great calamity.</p>
        </scopecontent>
        <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
             We combined search terms from both Wynn and Yitao. We added "17th century" 
             to help students filtering by time period, and "fires" as a broader topic 
             beyond just this specific event. -->
        <controlaccess>
          <corpname relator="creator"><part>The London Gazette</part></corpname>
          <subject><part>Great Fire of London, 1666</part></subject>
          <subject><part>fires</part></subject>
          <subject><part>london history</part></subject>
          <subject><part>17th century</part></subject>
          <genreform><part>newspaper reports</part></genreform>
          <geogname><part>London, England</part></geogname>
        </controlaccess>
        <userestrict>
          <p>Reuse conditions must be checked against The National Archives' copyright and image reuse guidance.</p>
        </userestrict>
      </c>

      <!-- 2. WRIGHT BROTHERS (Magda)                                -->
      <c level="item" id="se-1908-letter">
        <did>
          <unittitle>Wright Brothers 1908: letter to the War Office</unittitle>
          <unitid>se-1908-letter</unitid>
          <unitid localtype="tna-reference">AIR 1/728/176/3/33</unitid>
          <unitdatestructured unitdatetype="inclusive">
            <datesingle standarddate="1908-04-10">10 April 1908</datesingle>
          </unitdatestructured>
          
          <!-- RESEARCH & AUTHORSHIP DECISION (Wright Brothers)      -->
          <!-- RESEARCH FINDINGS: 
               Magda researched the letter and found it is signed "Wright Brothers" 
               with the initials "O.W." (Orville Wright) below.
               
               OUR APPROACH:
               We decided to create TWO separate <origination> tags, one for Wilbur 
               and one for Orville. We chose this because archival description 
               standards prioritize linking to specific individuals where possible. 
               This ensures that if a researcher searches for "Wilbur Wright", this 
               document is retrieved, even though the corporate name is on the letterhead.

               ALTERNATIVE APPROACH (Why we didn't do it):
               We *could* have used a single corporate name tag: 
               <origination><corpname><part>Wright Brothers</part></corpname></origination>
               This would have matched the letterhead exactly. However, we rejected 
               this because "Wright Brothers" is not a registered corporate entity in 
               the same way a government department is. Using individual persnames 
               provides much richer metadata for researchers. -->
          <origination>
            <persname><part>Wright, Wilbur</part></persname>
          </origination>
          <origination>
            <persname><part>Wright, Orville</part></persname>
          </origination>

          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Magda noted the letter is a typed copy ("(Copy)"). We explicitly 
               included this in the physfacet so users know they are not looking 
               at a handwritten original by the brothers. -->
          <physdescstructured physdescstructuredtype="materialtype" coverage="whole">
            <quantity>1</quantity>
            <unittype>letter</unittype>
            <physfacet>Typed copy of a letter on one page, marked "(Copy)", headed from the Wright Brothers, 1127 W. Third Street, Dayton, Ohio, and addressed to The Secretary, War Office, London, S.W.; signed "(sd) Wright Brothers" with the initials O.W. below</physfacet>
          </physdescstructured>
          <repository>
            <corpname><part>The National Archives</part></corpname>
          </repository>
          <langmaterial>
            <language langcode="eng">English</language>
          </langmaterial>
          <abstract>Copy of a letter dated 10 April 1908 from the American Wright brothers in Dayton, Ohio, to the Secretary of the War Office in London. It offers to make their two-man aeroplanes for military scouting, either under a contract or by licensing the British Government to build them under their British patent, and says the United States Government had recently placed a contract with them for one machine.</abstract>
          
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               We used the <dao> tag with <descriptivenote> to explain to users 
               that the link goes to a Web Archive version, ensuring they know 
               it is a snapshot of a historical TNA page. -->
          <dao daotype="derived" coverage="whole" label="Live TNA page: Wright Brothers 1908" href="https://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ukgwa/+/https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/significant-events/wright-brothers-1908/" linktitle="Wright Brothers 1908 - The National Archives" actuate="onrequest" show="new">
            <descriptivenote>
              <p>Public TNA Education page, accessed through the UK Government Web Archive, with the document image and a full transcript.</p>
            </descriptivenote>
          </dao>
          <didnote localtype="context">The TNA page describes the item as a letter from the American Wright brothers to the UK War Office about a possible contract to build their two-man aeroplanes.</didnote>
        </did>
        <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
             Full transcript preserved to maintain the exact wording of the 1908 
             proposal, including original punctuation. -->
        <scopecontent>
          <head>Transcript</head>
          <p>(Copy) Wright Brothers, 1127 W. Third Street, Dayton, Ohio.</p>
          <p>The Secretary, War Office, London, S.W.</p>
          <p>April 10, 1908.</p>
          <p>Dear Sir,</p>
          <p>The increased interest now taken in the different countries in aeronautics, prompts to again call your attention to our aeroplane. The machines as now designed, are suitable for military scouting, being capable of carrying two men (an operator and an observer) and sufficient fuel for long flights.</p>
          <p>We are prepared to undertake the manufacture of one or more of these machines under a contract; or, if the Government would prefer to build machines for itself under our British patent, we will grant license to operate on payment of suitable royalties on the machines so manufactured.</p>
          <p>The United States Government has lately entered into contract with us for one of our two-men machines.</p>
          <p>Very respectfully,</p>
          <p>(sd) Wright Brothers</p>
          <p>O.W.</p>
        </scopecontent>
        <controlaccess>
          <persname relator="creator"><part>Wright, Wilbur</part></persname>
          <persname relator="creator"><part>Wright, Orville</part></persname>
          <corpname relator="recipient"><part>Great Britain. War Office</part></corpname>
          <subject><part>aviation</part></subject>
          <subject><part>aeroplanes</part></subject>
          <subject><part>military aeronautics</part></subject>
          <subject><part>20th century</part></subject>
          <genreform><part>letters</part></genreform>
          <geogname><part>Dayton, Ohio, United States</part></geogname>
          <geogname><part>London, England</part></geogname>
        </controlaccess>
        <userestrict>
          <p>Refer to The National Archives website for reuse terms.</p>
        </userestrict>
      </c>

      <!-- 3. TITANIC (Dia) -->
      <c level="item" id="se-1912-telegram">
        <did>
          <unittitle>Titanic Distress Telegram 1912 received by S.S. Birma</unittitle>
          <unitid>se-1912-telegram</unitid>
          <unitid localtype="tna-reference">MT 9/920C</unitid>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Dia researched the timeline of the sinking. The distress call was sent 
               late at night on April 14th and received in the early hours of April 15th. 
               We used a <daterange> instead of a single date to capture this exact 
               historical timeline accurately. -->
          <unitdatestructured unitdatetype="inclusive">
            <daterange>
              <fromdate standarddate="1912-04-14">14 April 1912</fromdate>
              <todate standarddate="1912-04-15">15 April 1912</todate>
            </daterange>
          </unitdatestructured>
          <origination>
            <corpname><part>Russian East Asiatic S.S. Co.</part></corpname>
          </origination>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Dia included the physical stamps and numbers (M16307, 526) found on 
               the document. This is vital for archival research, as it allows 
               users to identify the exact physical copy held by TNA. -->
          <physdescstructured physdescstructuredtype="materialtype" coverage="whole">
            <quantity>1</quantity>
            <unittype>radio-telegram</unittype>
            <physfacet>Typed on a printed Russian East Asiatic S.S. Co. form, with a table of columns for: number of words, origin station, time handed in, route and remarks; stamped M16307 and numbered 526</physfacet>
          </physdescstructured>
          <repository>
            <corpname><part>The National Archives</part></corpname>
          </repository>
          <langmaterial>
            <language langcode="eng">English</language>
          </langmaterial>
          <abstract>Radio-telegram sent on the night of 14/15 April 1912 at 11h45m from the Titanic (call sign MGY) and received by the S.S. Birma. It records a distress call saying the ship had struck an iceberg, was sinking fast and was asking for assistance at the position stated.</abstract>
          <dao daotype="derived" href="https://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ukgwa/+/https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/significant-events/titanic-telegram-1912/" actuate="onrequest" show="new"/>
        </did>
        <scopecontent>
          <head>Transcript</head>
          <p>The Russian East Asiatic S.S. Co. Radio-Telegram. S.S. "Birma".</p>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Dia recreated the physical table printed on the form using XML 
               <table> tags. This is an advanced archival description technique 
               that preserves the layout and structure of the original document, 
               not just the text. -->
          <table frame="all">
            <tgroup cols="5">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>No. Words</entry>
                  <entry>Origin Station</entry>
                  <entry>Time handed in</entry>
                  <entry>Via</entry>
                  <entry>Remarks</entry>
                </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>bg to 6.</entry>
                  <entry>Titanic</entry>
                  <entry>11 H.45M. April 14/15 1912.</entry>
                  <entry/> <!-- Empty entry to represent the blank "Via" column -->
                  <entry>Distress call Ligs Loud.</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
          <p>Cgd - SOS. from M. G. Y. We have struck iceberg sinking fast come to our assistance. Position Lat. 41.46 n. Lon. 50.14. W. M. G. Y.</p>
          <p>[MGY was the codename for the Titanic.]</p>
        </scopecontent>
        <controlaccess>
          <corpname relator="sender"><part>Titanic</part></corpname>
          <corpname relator="recipient"><part>Birma</part></corpname>
          <subject><part>titanic</part></subject>
          <subject><part>shipwrecks</part></subject>
          <subject><part>20th century</part></subject>
          <genreform><part>telegrams</part></genreform>
          <geogname><part>North Atlantic</part></geogname>
        </controlaccess>
        <userestrict>
          <p>Refer to The National Archives website for reuse terms.</p>
        </userestrict>
      </c>

      <!-- 4. NHS (Ivana) -->
      <c level="item" id="item-nhs-1948">
        <did>
          <unittitle>Birth of the NHS (1948): "Your New National Health Service" advertisement</unittitle>
          <unitid>item-nhs-1948</unitid>
          <unitid localtype="tna-reference">INF 2/66</unitid>
          <unitdatestructured unitdatetype="inclusive">
            <datesingle standarddate="1948">1948</datesingle>
          </unitdatestructured>
          <origination>
            <corpname><part>Scotland. Department of Health</part></corpname>
          </origination>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Ivana noted this was a public information leaflet, not a standard 
               government file. Describing it as a "Printed single-page public 
               information advertisement" helps users understand its intended 
               audience (the general public, not internal government use). -->
          <physdescstructured physdescstructuredtype="materialtype" coverage="whole">
            <quantity>1</quantity>
            <unittype>advertisement</unittype>
            <physfacet>Printed single-page public information advertisement, digitised as an image</physfacet>
          </physdescstructured>
          <repository>
            <corpname><part>The National Archives</part></corpname>
          </repository>
          <langmaterial>
            <language langcode="eng">English</language>
          </langmaterial>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Ivana's abstract includes the specific date (5 July 1948), the form 
               number (E.C.1), and the publication context (selected Scottish 
               newspapers). This level of detail is excellent for educational 
               resources, giving students all the key facts at a glance. -->
          <abstract>A public information advertisement issued by the Department of Health for Scotland to explain that the National Health Service would start on 5 July 1948. It says that anyone can use the service, with no age limits and no fees, and tells people to choose a doctor and to collect application form E.C.1 for each family member. It appeared in selected Sunday, morning and evening newspapers in Scotland.</abstract>
          <dao daotype="derived" coverage="whole" href="https://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ukgwa/+/https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/significant-events/birth-of-the-nhs-1948/" linktitle="Birth of the NHS 1948 - The National Archives" actuate="onrequest" show="new"/>
        </did>
        <scopecontent>
          <p>Advertisement that introduces the new National Health Service to the public in Scotland shortly before it began. It explains who can use the service, how it is paid for through public funds and how people should register with a doctor.</p>
        </scopecontent>
        <controlaccess>
          <corpname relator="creator"><part>Scotland. Department of Health</part></corpname>
          <subject><part>National Health Service (Great Britain)</part></subject>
          <subject><part>health services</part></subject>
          <subject><part>public health</part></subject>
          <subject><part>20th century</part></subject>
          <genreform><part>advertisements</part></genreform>
          <geogname><part>Scotland</part></geogname>
        </controlaccess>
        <userestrict>
          <p>Refer to The National Archives website for reuse terms.</p>
        </userestrict>
      </c>

      <!-- 5. MOON LANDING (Lesong) -->
      <c level="item" id="se-1969">
        <did>
          <unittitle>Moon Landing (1969)</unittitle>
          <unitid>se-1969</unitid>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               We added the TNA reference (FCO 7/1018) to ensure consistency 
               across all five records, as the other four had their references 
               explicitly stated. -->
          <unitid localtype="tna-reference">FCO 7/1018</unitid>
          <unitdatestructured unitdatetype="inclusive">
            <datesingle standarddate="1969-07-21">21 July 1969</datesingle>
          </unitdatestructured>
          <origination>
            <corpname><part>Foreign and Commonwealth Office</part></corpname>
          </origination>
          <!-- RESEARCH RATIONALE: 
               Lesong researched the TNA catalogue and discovered the document 
               is marked "RESTRICTED". We added this to the physfacet to highlight 
               the document's security classification at the time of creation. -->
          <physdescstructured physdescstructuredtype="materialtype" coverage="whole">
            <quantity>1</quantity>
            <unittype>telegram</unittype>
            <physfacet>Restricted flash telegram from the Foreign and Commonwealth Office to Washington, containing a message from the Prime Minister.</physfacet>
          </physdescstructured>
          <repository>
            <corpname><part>The National Archives</part></corpname>
          </repository>
          <langmaterial>
            <language langcode="eng">English</language>
          </langmaterial>
          <abstract>Telegram from the Foreign and Commonwealth Office to Washington, 21 July 1969, conveying Prime Minister Harold Wilson’s congratulations to President Nixon and the Apollo 11 astronauts on the successful Moon landing.</abstract>
          <dao daotype="derived" href="https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/significant-events/moon-landing-1969/" actuate="onrequest" show="new"/>
        </did>
        <scopecontent>
          <p>Educational resource based on a restricted telegram about the Apollo 11 mission.</p>
        </scopecontent>
